keys:

    ctrl+t (or ctrl+n)      new shell tab
    ctrl+w                  close tab
    ctrl+→/← (or ctrl+f/b)  next / previous tab


install to `~/.local/bin/tabs`:

    curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sueszli/tmux-tabs/master/install.sh | bash


update with:

    curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sueszli/tmux-tabs/master/update.sh | bash


make sure `~/.local/bin` is on your path, then run `tabs`.
to use `tabs` when you type `tmux`, add `alias tmux=tabs` to your shell config.
`command tmux` still runs the real tmux.


many machines, one session:

a tmux server lives on the machine that started it, so on a cluster of login
nodes you reach a different one every time you log in. list the machines you
share a home directory with and `tabs` keeps a single session for you:

    # ~/.config/tabs/hosts
    login01.example.edu
    login02.example.edu
    login03.example.edu

the first `tabs` starts the session on whichever listed host answers with the
lowest load average, so a hammered node is skipped. every later `tabs` finds
that session again and reconnects to it over ssh, from any of the machines.
the list can also come from `$TABS_HOSTS`, and with no list configured `tabs`
just runs where you are.

    tabs --pick     print the host that would be chosen
    tabs --local    stay on this machine and remember it

this needs ssh between the hosts to work without a password.
where logind sets `KillUserProcesses=yes`, the server is started in its own
systemd scope so it outlives the login that created it.
